kmore frier aye kesler drobny schleifer buckingham neuropsychologic neuroanatomic semenkovich doty siller arbelaez jessup grimley passmore belger calikoglu nadebaum northam koves finney wellard inder dkabis duinkerken rosano northam rankins werther kirchhoff jundt doty cato mauras mazaika kollman aye ambrosino ruedy tansey cato mauras ambrosino bondurant kollman ruedy aye marzelli mazaika barnea goraly tsalikian tamborlane mauras buckingham ruedy kollman dysglycemia mauras mazaika buckingham weinzimer tsalikian cato kollman ruedy aye arbelaez tansey tamborlane marzelli winer saggar tsalikian mauras mazaika weinzimer buckingham hyperconnectivity thilen nordenstrom hagenfeldt dobeln guthenbery gidlof wedell guthenbery dobeln nordenstrom nordenstrom wedell hagenfeldt hydroxyprogesterone cyp21 e68 nordenstrom thilen hagenfeldt wedell mirela nader amelio bachega p32 carbalho madureira marcondes billerbeck presti kuperman damiani bachega cyp21a2 merke poppas merke endocrin merke culter zimmet alberti tajima silink arslanian caprio brumbaugh crume nadeau scherzinger dabelea intramyocellular e1099 e1105 alias galera vico olvera porcel bonillo perales insulinaemia jeffery metcalf hosking streeter wilkin earlybird weitzman auinger viner lichtarowicz krynska hindmarsh ahrens marild siani henauw bohmann hadjigeorgiou iacoviello lissner veidebaum pohlabeln pigeot idefics idefics dziura burgert tamborlane taksali yeckel savoye sherwin caprio efstathiou skeva zorbala georgiou mountokalakis prema zamrazilova hainer aldhoon hainerova wabitsch rothenbacher schimmelmann moß lennerz weyermann rothenbacher wabitsch ekelund linne neovius brage dunger wareham rossner whincup lustig bartok stifter mallan nambiar mauch magarey e40 e49 grote closa monasterolo escribano langhendries dain giovannini verduci gruszfeld socha koletzko lustig noworolski erkin cakmak gugliucci isocaloric kusminski taksali caprio dziura cali papademetris burgert pierpont savoye seyal adamo cali amg pierpont northrup caprio kursawe eszlinger bazuine cali amg adamo pierpont cushman caprio adipogenic savoye nowicka dziura chavent serrecchia tamborlane caprio jungner bol oficina sanit panam deladoey ruel giguere schoen clapp fireman isotopic e683 e688 cielonko hamby kaplowitz sundararajan olivieri torresani krude polak slep jspe apeg appes ispae grosse','The PES Topic Symposium on Diabetes and Brain was co-chaired by Esko Wiltshire, MD (University of Otago, Wellington, New Zealand) and Joseph Wolfsdorf, MD (Boston Children’s Hospital, Boston, MA, USA). The first speaker in the session was Fergus Cameron, MD (Royal Children’s Hospital Melbourne, Melbourne, Australia), who gave a presentation entitled “Type 1 diabetes and the developing brain: Mechanisms of injury.” He discussed how glucose is the primary fuel for the brain and that type 1 diabetes (T1DM) is the “exemplary condition of disordered glucose homeostasis”. A Pittsburgh study of 97 adults with T1DM showed a five times higher prevalence of clinically relevant cognitive impairment compared to controls ( 1 ). Those with diabetes have a reduction of brain volume ( 2 ) which may be linked to these cognitive impairments. Why does this occur and what factors influence this? In this presentation, Dr. Cameron discussed how various elements, notably hypoglycemia, hyperglycemia, severe hypoglycemia, and DKA, may impact cognition and brain development and function. Regarding the impact of glucose variability or day-to-day episodes of hypoglycemia and hyperglycemia, one study in school-aged children with T1DM showed that blood glucose values of 72–270 mg/dL (4–15 mM) is the “sweet spot” for cognitive function ( 3 ). Outside of this range, there was a deficit seen in performance. According to these published results, “Math time was an average of 12.6 and 16.8 sec longer when blood glucose levels were <54 mg/dL (<3.0 mM) and >400 mg/dL (>22.2 mM), respectively. Reaction time was an average of 2.6 and 1.5 sec slower when glucose levels were <54 mg/dL (<3.0 mM) and >400 mg/dL (>22.2 mM), respectively.” Dr. Cameron then discussed the impact of severe hypoglycemia (that causing seizure or coma) on brain development and cognition. Severe hypoglycemia has been associated in some previously published studies with poorer IQ outcomes, with a cumulative effect ( 4 ). Studies have shown that severe hypoglycemia is associated with grey and white matter volume changes ( 5 ). The study examining this variation was unable to look at impact of recurrent hypoglycemia (i.e., cumulative effect) given sample size limitations, but saw a reduction in grey and white matter in children with T1DM with any hypoglycemic seizure history compared to children with T1DM with no such history. Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) may have an impact on brain development as well. Studies have demonstrated that DKA is correlated with negative cognitive outcomes ( 6 , 7 ). Dr. Cameron’s group performed a study of brain injury at the time of diabetes diagnosis, comparing 36 children with DKA on presentation compared to 59 matched control patients (no DKA at T1DM initial presentation), excluding those with evidence of cerebral edema ( 8 ). These subjects were studied within 48 hours of diagnosis, and then 5 days, 28 days, and 6 months later. Those with DKA at diagnosis had evidence of swollen white matter compared to controls, particularly in the frontal, temporal, and parietal lobes. The DKA subjects also had a decreased volume of grey matter. These white- and grey-matter changes were seen acutely, but resolved over time. However, volume changes seen in the first 5 days were associated with poorer cognitive outcomes at 6 months related to delayed memory recall and attention. Dr. Cameron concluded his talk with a call for more research in this area, particularly related to possible interventions to prevent these changes in brain development. Neuroprotective factors may include preventing DKA at diagnosis, reducing glycemic variations, encouraging exercise, and implementing educational and cognitive processing interventions. The second speaker was Tamara Hershey, PhD (Washington University, St. Louis, MO, USA), who spoke on cognitive development in children with T1DM. Diabetes mellitus (type 1 or type 2) is a risk factor for cognitive impairment ( 9 ). Dr. Hershey described the need to evaluate how various factors influence this risk. Childhood-onset T1DM may have worse cognitive outcomes. During childhood, the developing brain is dynamic, and this dynamic structure is exposed to glycemic extremes with T1DM. This poses the question of how these neurodevelopmental systems interact with fluctuating glucose supply. Factors that influence this interaction may include: age at diagnosis, frequency of episodes of DKA, severe hypoglycemia, co-morbidities (such as mental health conditions), and available educational support and resources, often related to socioeconomic status (SES). Dr. Hershey pointed out there are methodological issues in the current literature because of different metrics used for glycemic exposure, different tests of cognitive assessment, limited diversity of patients, small sample sizes, and often no baseline analyses available. There are also diversity issues because of the use of homogenous samples with above-average SES and intelligence quotients (IQs) in most cohorts. Lower SES and non-Caucasian ethnicity have been associated with poorer glycemic control, but few studies adequately include these groups in their research population. In a review of literature in this area, Dr. Hershey summarized data from three meta-analyses and three cohort studies: Northam, Hershey, and DirecNet. The meta-analyses are all cross-sectional in nature and universally showed that youth with T1DM had lower IQs, motor speed, and attention compared to controls, but with small effect sizes. Studies were inconsistent regarding the effect of severe hypoglycemia and impact of age of onset. In the Northam cohort, 106 youth with T1DM were enrolled close to the time of diagnosis of their diabetes ( 10 ). This cohort has been followed for over 12 years and enlisted controls from the community. By 3 months after diagnosis, there were no changes in cognitive function in those with T1DM compared with controls. However, over time, there was emergence of differences in IQ, attention, and working memory. In this cohort, the poorest performing children had evidence of multiple “hits” with increased rates of hypoglycemia and/or hyperglycemia exposure and younger age of onset of their diabetes. The Hershey cohorts consist of separate newly diagnosed and longer diagnosed T1DM cohorts, with control groups of siblings without diabetes ( 11 ). Subjects were asked to perform a spatial delayed response task in addition to a cognitive battery. In the newly diagnosed cohort, DKA and degree of hyperglycemia (measured as HbA1c) at diagnosis produced more error on the memory task at the 3-month time-point ( 6 ). The longitudinal cohort showed that youth with diabetes performed worse than sibling controls on visual-spatial ability and memory tasks over time ( 11 ). Additionally, decreases in frequency of hyperglycemia over time demonstrated individual improvements in visual-spatial skills and processing speeds. The multi-site DirecNet cohort includes youth with T1DM between 4–10 years of age and non-diabetic controls ( 12 , 13 ). At baseline, with an average duration of diabetes of 3 years, the T1DM group had lower IQ, poorer executive function, and greater internalizing symptoms (anxiety and depression-type issues). Participants with a history of DKA and severe hypoglycemia in the past had a negative effect on IQ measures at baseline assessment. Additionally, hyperglycemia (HbA1c area-under-the-curve >6.0%) negatively impacted IQ, learning, and memory. However, some differences were not sustained over time as 18 months later, there were no IQ differences between the T1DM group and the non-T1DM control group. Within the T1DM group, some glycemic variables continued to demonstrate relationships 18 months later. DKA was correlated with lower verbal IQ and increased HbA1c was correlated with poorer executive function. This cohort is still being followed and, hopefully, data will be available in the future with regard to how these patients have fared over time. In summary, Dr. Hershey concluded that affected cognitive domains in T1DM are widespread, but subtle. These are apparent early in the course of the disease, with changes also detected over time. The final speaker of the session was Allan Reiss, MD (Stanford University, Palo Alto, CA, USA), who spoke on neuroanatomical and neuro-functional consequences of T1DM in children. He discussed publications from the DirecNet study on neuroanatomical correlates of glycemic dysregulation in children with T1DM ( 13 , 14 ). When comparing T1DM subjects with controls in terms of whole-brain volumetric analysis using voxel-based morphology (VBM) determined by MRI, some regions of the brain in those with T1DM had increased grey-matter volume involving the left inferior prefrontal, insula, and temporal pole, while the occipital and cerebellar regions bilaterally had decreased grey-matter volume. The DirecNet study has also shown that both grey matter and white matter develop more slowly over time in children with T1DM compared to controls when comparing baseline (average diabetes duration of 3 years) and 18-month follow-up studies ( 15 ). Greater hyperglycemia and higher glucose variability were particularly associated with these changes in development. To measure brain function, a 4D MRI image can be done, with time as the fourth dimension, which is referred to as a functional MRI (fMRI). When looking at resting-state functional connectivity, fMRI showed increased connectivity in the brains of children with T1DM compared with controls ( 16 ). Increased connectivity was correlated with higher cognitive scores in the T1DM group. This study suggests a degree of compensatory hyper-connectivity in children with T1DM compared to controls without T1DM that may be due to potential underlying structural defects, given the neuroanatomical differences in brain volumes seen in prior studies as discussed earlier. Dr. Reiss and colleagues are also currently looking at how brain activation patterns during executive function tasks may be impacted by diabetes. In summary, Dr. Reiss concluded that, related to typically developing children, children with T1DM show sustained differences in brain development over time, and that perhaps overall hyperglycemia and glucose variability are related to these differences. Children with T1DM also show differences in brain function that may be related to their underlying disease, be compensatory in nature, or result from disease-environmental interaction related to how living with a chronic disease and the need for continuous surveillance of blood glucose may impact response patterns and level of brain activity/connectivity. Furthermore, changes in brain structure and function can predate clinically significant findings in cognition and behavior. Overall, the Diabetes and the Brain symposium offered a wealth of information from experts in the field. It provided a review of the literature, as well as a preview of where research in this exciting area is going. Much of this is uncharted territory and our understanding of the mechanisms, impacts, and interventions related to diabetes and the developing brain will surely expand over the coming years as more research is done in this area. The PES topic symposium entitled: “New Concepts in Diagnosis and Treatment of CAH,” was co-chaired by Xiaoping Luo, MD (Department of Pediatrics, Tongji Hospital, Wuhan, China) and Hugo Boquete, MD (Department of Medicine, Hospital T. Alvarez, Buenos Aires, Argentina). The first speaker was Anna Nordenstrom, MD (Department of Pediatrics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden) who introduced the international (from Sweden) and longitudinal perspective on neonatal screening for CAH. Based on her previous studies, the prevalence by screening of CAH in Sweden was 1:9800 ( 17 ). Patients with CAH were identified earlier by screening. In the study population, 25% of the girls and 73% of the boys were diagnosed by screening alone. The median age at the time of the definitive diagnosis in boys was 21 days before screening as compared with 9 days after initiation screening. The false-positive rate was <0.05% and, in about 60% of cases, it was attributable to prematurity. From the recent 26-year longitudinal population study, a total of 143 patients with salt-wasting CAH were identified and none was missed ( 18 ). The sensitivity was lower for milder forms, including 79.7% for simple-virilizing forms and 32.4% for non-classical forms. The positive predictive value was higher in full-term (25.1%) than in preterm (1.4%) infants and correlated with gestational age. The recall rate in full-term infants (0.03%) was lower than that in preterm infants (0.57%). Screening for CAH was highly effective in detecting the salt-wasting form and, thus, reducing mortality. It was emphasized that the benefits and importance of universal screening are to avoid serious salt-losing crises, to provide earlier correct gender assignment, and to detect patients who would have otherwise been missed in the neonatal period. Studies have questioned the rationale of neonatal screening for CAH owing to a high rate of recall (i.e., false-positive findings), especially in preterm infants ( 19 ). In the past, 17-hydroxyprogesterone (17-OHP) was analyzed by radioimmunoassay and cut-off plasma levels were gestational age-related. With the introduction of newer methodology, the cut-off level was adjusted to increase sensitivity. However, neonatal screening for CAH among preterm infants was complicated by the fact that healthy pre-term infants have higher levels of 17-OHP than do term infants, resulting in a higher false-positive rate. Pre-term infants had a different cut-off after ether extraction. Even when gestational age-related cut-off levels after ether extraction were used, the false-positive cases primarily comprised preterm infants. Thus, the ether extraction method was subsequently abandoned because it delayed the result without considerably increasing sensitivity. By this method, the positive predictive value was approximately 14.3% and 1% in term and preterm infants respectively. It also allowed increased detection of non-classical CAH with gestational age cut-off levels. Of note, boys also demonstrated higher 17-OHP levels in infancy than in girls. Next, Dr. Nordenstrom discussed the pros and cons of second-tier analysis. It may create anxiety for the family and additional cost for running new samples. There has been discussion on additional analyses of the same sample to increase the sensitivity by LCMS. The calculated ratio of the sum of 17-OHP and androstenedione divided by cortisol has been used for confirming the diagnosis. It has been suggested that a ratio >2.5 was highly diagnostic. However, the data suggest the possibility of missing 15% of salt-wasting cases by using this method. She recommended starting treatment early if screening demonstrated an elevated 17-OHP level to prevent a salt-wasting crisis and, thus, minimize potential neonatal morbidity and mortality. Dr. Nordenstrom also presented the long-term health outcomes for patients with CAH based on the Swedish experience. The main causes of mortality were adrenal crisis and cardiovascular disease. There has been reported an increased risk of cancers, psychiatric disorders, and higher rates of suicide, particularly in female patients with less education. Thus, it is important to provide comprehensive care and treatment to prevent unfavorable long-term health consequences. She also emphasized the importance of improving patients’ quality of life to minimize adverse health outcomes and complications. The next speaker was Berenice Mendonca, MD, PhD (Universidade de Sao Paulo, Sao Paulo, Brazil). She shared the current data on hormonal and molecular diagnosis of CAH in clinical practice. There are various subtypes of CAH depending on the enzyme defect in the pathway. Metabolites are helpful in differentiating particular enzymatic defects particularly after ACTH stimulation. For example, with 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (HSD) deficiency, ACTH-stimulated pregnenolone and DHEA levels are elevated. Elevated levels of deoxycorticosterone (DOC) and corticosterone are found in patients with 17-hydroxylase deficiency. She also suggested that ACTH-stimulated 17-OHP levels could predict carrier status for severe mutations of the gene encoding 21-hydroxylase. Thus, careful evaluation of the levels of specific hormonal intermediates could offer diagnostic assistance. Other available diagnostic methods include urinary steroid profiles. However, this method may not be readily available in every country. Several subtypes of CAH share similar hormonal profiles that might not be easily differentiable. In addition, stressed newborns could present with persistently increased confirmatory hormone levels, sometimes requiring prolonged follow-up. Thus, there might be utility for molecular analysis to improve CAH diagnosis. Dr Mendonca presented data on incorporating molecular analysis from the newborn screening program in Brazil ( 20 ). In newborns with increased levels of 17-OHP on screening and confirmatory venous samples, DNA was extracted and genotyping was performed by allele-specific polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and multiplex ligation-dependent probe amplification (MLPA) techniques; entire gene sequencing was performed when necessary. Among all newborns with positive NBS, 57% had genotypes predicting classical forms and 83% the salt-wasting type. Genotyping identified mutations in all newborns who screened positive ( 21 ). Several available methods for molecular tests were used, e.g. Sanger sequencing, target massive parallel sequencing, and exome sequencing, to identify the defective genetic components. She concluded that molecular testing was a useful supplemental tool especially in differentiating false from true positive results in CAH, thus preventing unnecessary follow-up of cases with inconclusive hormonal tests. Her group observed a good genotype/phenotype correlation that can be helpful to predict the clinical subtype in asymptomatic affected newborns and anticipate disease severity ( 22 ). The last speaker was Deborah Merke, MD (National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, MD, USA) who discussed new treatment approaches for CAH. Dr Merke provided data showing that many of the adverse outcomes in patients with CAH are attributable to suboptimal disease control. There is a high risk of adrenal insufficiency related to under-treatment or patient unawareness. Patients who are suboptimally managed are also at risk of developing hyperandrogenism due to undertreatment or hypercorticolism due to overtreatment ( 23 ). Poor health outcomes include stunted growth, virilization, and infertility. It has been reported that patients with CAH who undergo in vitro fertilization (IVF) had poor outcome with increased risk of hypertension, pre-eclampsia, and prematurity. Adrenal insufficiency is the major cause of death for patients with CAH. It is advisable to increase adrenal insufficiency awareness by education and use of an emergency card. Dr. Merke also stressed the need for improved monitoring of the treatment response using more sensitive markers. Conventional androgens, such as androstenedione and DHEA, poorly correlate with hyperandrogenism. She suggested that the newer marker, 11-oxygenated androgen (11oxC19), from adrenal cortex might better reflect long-term control of patients with 21-hydroxylase deficiency. There are new treatment options currently under investigation to improve compliance as well as to deliver glucocorticoid more physiologically ( 24 , 25 ). The goals of the new treatment modalities are to optimize growth, prevent co-morbidities, and minimize glucocorticoid and androgen exposures. The mainstay of treatment remains glucocorticoid replacement. A modified-release capsule of hydrocortisone given twice daily has led to improved glucocorticoid and androgen control. In addition, it decreased the frequency of medication dosing and improved adherence. Glucocorticoid delivered via subcutaneous pump has also been used in patients whose CAH is difficult to control manifested by significantly high 17-OHP and androstenedione levels. Patients could titrate their dose based on symptoms and serum or salivary cortisol levels. Individuals treated by pump have reported improved quality of life, including less fatigue and more vitality. Apart from conventional glucocorticoid replacement, it has been suggested that combining agents that inhibit ACAT1, the enzyme that transforms free cholesterol into cholesterol ester, and thereby blocks steroidogenesis at any early stage; interfere with androgen biosynthesis, such as abiraterone acetate that inhibits 17-hydroxylase; antagonize CRH; and are adrenolytic, such as mitotane. By directly minimizing the degree of hyperandrogenism, this could allow a reduction in the dose of glucocorticoid diminishing its likelihood of causing unfavorable side effects. Despite the various new treatment approaches for CAH being developed and/or undergoing clinical trials aimed at improving treatment outcomes and reducing co-morbidities, the speakers emphasized the importance of early disease identification and intervention as a means to prevent untoward complications of the condition and/or its treatment. The PES Topic Symposium entitled: “Metabolic Syndrome in Early Childhood” was chaired by Francesco Chiarelli, MD, PhD (University of Chieti, Chieti, Italy). The first speaker, Ram Weiss, MD (Hebrew University School of Medicine, Jerusalem, Israel), in his lecture entitled: “Definition of metabolic syndrome in toddlers and children”, focused on the current definitions of metabolic syndrome (MetS) and their limitations, as well as strategies to assess metabolic risk in younger children. He started by recapitulating the International Diabetes Federation (IDF) consensus definition ( 26 ), which limits the possibility of diagnosing MetS below 10 years of age. The pathophysiology of MetS in adolescents is due to underlying obesity, which leads to increased production of adipocytokines from adipose tissue followed by development of target-organ resistance to insulin. He stated the same principle holds true in younger children, as reported in two major studies. The first is the EPOCH study ( 27 ) in which intramyocellular lipid accumulation (IMCL) was found to be associated with visceral adiposity and insulin resistance in pre-pubertal and early pubertal children. The second study by Alías-Hernández et al ( 28 ) of 459 pre-pubertal Caucasian children between 2–10 years age reported that the incidence of insulin resistance was significantly increased with abdominal obesity and high waist circumference. Although puberty is associated with a 33% reduction in insulin sensitivity, it is questionable if the degree is truly as high in cases of pre-existing extreme obesity. He reported that, specifically in healthy non-obese girls, serum insulin levels start to rise before the onset of puberty as noted by a study by Jeffery et al ( 29 ). Thus, the concept that puberty is the main risk factor for insulin resistance may not be entirely correct. He next compared various proposed definitions for MetS and their limitations. The IDF consensus definition ( 26 ) uses high cut-offs for blood pressure and the Cook ( 30 ) and Viner ( 31 ) definitions have high cut-offs for glucose/insulin levels, thus resulting in differential weighting of the components of the syndrome. The IDEFICS definition ( 32 ) uses HOMA-IR, but this can only be compared when biochemical measurements are done in the same laboratory and, thus, cannot be generalized. Dr. Weiss next talked about his own modified definition ( 33 ) which employs BMI >97 th percentile for age, triglyceride level, systolic blood pressure >95 th percentile, and HDL cholesterol <5 th percentile, all age- and gender-adjusted. Importantly, this definition uses impaired glucose tolerance (post-glucose load plasma glucose of >140 mg/dL, but < 200 mg/dL) rather than fasting glucose. The IDF definition, though, is restricted to children >10 years and employs 90 th percentile cut-offs, is easy to use, and can be compared across different centers, as compared to the Weiss definition which is age- and sex-adjusted, but includes the more severe obese phenotype as it uses BMI >97 th percentile. Another difference is that family history of overweight is used in the Weiss criteria, but not in the IDF model. Age and gender do not affect the Weiss model as compared to the IDF criteria where, the older the age, the higher the risk of diagnosing MetS. These definitions are missing the components from early childhood that may contribute to MetS. The PREMA study ( 34 ) showed that small-for-gestational-age (SGA) infants are at significantly higher at risk for MetS even before they become obese. He recognized three criteria for developing MetS in adolescents: Birth weight <10 th percentile (having been born SGA) Birth head circumference <10th percentile Parental obesity Each criterion independently predicted development of MetS even before obesity was present. He reports that body composition appears to be the most important risk factor for development of a metabolic phenotype, with the risk for unhealthy fat storage already determined in utero . Regarding proposed components of MetS, the fasting glucose concentration is not useful at a young age, and he felt that the 2-hour glucose level was more helpful. The diagnosis of MetS does not contribute much clinically in young children. During this window, it is probably best to think about each component individually as not all components of MetS are equal predictors of future type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM). In the study by Zamrazilova, et al ( 35 ), two groups of obese adolescents matched for age, weight, height, and BMI were enrolled, one that was metabolically unhealthy (with at least three risk factors, including hypertension, dyslipidemia, and dysglycemia) and a second group that was metabolically healthy. A significant predictor in the metabolically unhealthy group for future cardiovascular risk was related to earlier onset and the duration of exposure to obesity. He summarized by saying that the pathophysiological mechanisms of MetS are not age-dependent and they do not differ with the onset of puberty. Insulin resistance is a key driver of the syndrome and it begins prior to and worsens during puberty. Non-traditional risk factors including having been born SGA, small head circumference, and family history of MetS should be included in the definition. Lastly, the average effect of obesity is time-dependent: the longer one is exposed to obesity, the more severe the future metabolic phenotype becomes. Dr. Weiss concluded by asking if it is critical to have a specific definition for MetS in children under age 10 years. He stated that, if he were to define MetS in young children, obesity (assessed as waist circumference or BMI >95 th percentile) would be a pre-requisite with additional major and minor criteria as follows: major criteria: high triglycerides, low HDL, impaired glucose tolerance (preferably a 2-hr post prandial glucose level, although it can be difficult to obtain), or high systolic blood pressure; and minor criteria: SGA, small head circumference, and age of onset of obesity, family history of MetS, or T2DM. He stated that further research is needed to determine how much significance should be given to each component. Martin Wabitsch, MD, PhD (University Medical Center, Ulm, Germany) began his talk entitled: “Prevention of metabolic syndrome in early childhood” by describing the risk factors and rationale for early prevention and intervention. There is a clustering and tracking of risk factors for MetS that are acquired early in life. Thus, it is important to prevent these aggravating factors in three life phases: Pre-conception and pregnancy Infancy and early childhood School age and adolescence MetS develops when there is excess food intake in the setting of a predisposed genetic background, along with lifestyle influence of poor dietary choices and physical exercise. This leads to an increase in body fat, with insulin resistance or hyperinsulinemia as the central part of MetS. There is a debate on which comes first. A recent review published in Nature Medicine ( 36 ) presented the following viewpoints. The first says that insulin resistance is primary, causing hyperinsulinemia. With a high-fat diet, hepatic gluconeogenesis is not suppressed by insulin. This causes increased glucose output and decreased glucose uptake by the muscle. Resulting hyperglycemia stimulates pancreatic beta cells and leads to chronic hyperinsulinemia. The second viewpoint is that hyperinsulinemia causes insulin resistance. In this model, a high-fat diet and overfeeding stimulate pancreatic beta cells to overproduce insulin causing a primary hyperinsulinemia. This leads to activation of inflammatory pathways that impair insulin responses in target tissues causing insulin resistance. There are five risk factors in early life that predispose to development of metabolic syndrome: Obesity: According to his Ulm Birth cohort study, longitudinal data over 11 years showed that established overweight at age 8 years already predicted BMI scores at age 11 years ( 37 ). Thus, obesity interventions should be implemented in early childhood as it is much more difficult to reduce overweight than to prevent its onset. Genetic factors: This is true because there is clustering of MetS in families. Inborn metabolic programming due to epigenetic changes leading to differential expression of genes related to metabolism: The Barker hypothesis states that our first influences in fetal life lead to late-onset adult chronic diseases. In pregnancy, transfer of glucose and amino acids through the placenta stimulates the fetal beta cells to secrete insulin leading to programming of the hypothalamic set point for insulin secretion. Postnatally, glucose and amino acids act directly on the pancreatic beta cells leading to insulin secretion depending on the prenatal set point. In pregnancy, the first two trimesters are an anabolic phase for the mother and there is increased fat accumulation in the mother. In the third trimester, there is an increase in maternal insulin resistance, a catabolic phase for the mother, and increased fat accumulation in the fetus. When the mother is obese, then throughout pregnancy there is a catabolic phase, leading to increased transport of nutrients to the fetus and beta cells in particular, and thus prenatal programming of the set point of insulin secretion. In the longitudinal population-based Ulm birth cohort study ( 38 ), fasting insulin levels in the eighth year of life were determined mainly by the BMI of the mother before pregnancy. This led to differentiation of two cohorts: children with fasting hyperinsulinemia and children with normal fasting insulin levels. Interestingly, children with higher insulin levels had higher BMIs throughout childhood starting in the first year of life and higher fatty acids levels compared to the cohort with normal insulin levels. Those children with high insulin levels developed obesity and those with normal insulin levels did not. Early weight gain: In SGA babies, early weight gain is an important risk factor for MetS, as their beta cells might be programmed for insulin resistance in utero . A Swedish study ( 39 ) showed that, in the first 6 months of life, rapid weight gain leads to increased MetS risk at age 17 years. This again emphasizes the need for early intervention. Early nutrition: Evidence from early nutrition studies suggests that high energy and high protein intake in infancy, as well as high consumption of sweetened drinks, is associated with heightened childhood obesity risk and MetS ( 40 – 42 ). Dr. Wabistch then talked about the prevention of MetS and the potential for intervention, which can be done in all three phases of life: Prenatal environment: We should aim to decrease the exposure to maternal diabetes mellitus and prevent SGA status ( 40 , 42 ). Infancy and early childhood: We should focus on parent-child interactions regarding food and activity. Improving infant formula (sugar content, energy content, and amount and quality of protein) should be undertaken to prevent future obesity. In a randomized controlled trial ( 43 ), 698 mothers of healthy term infants were randomized either to receive usual care or 12 weeks of group education focusing on increased exposure to healthy groups, responsive feeding, and positive parenting. Results showed that anticipatory guidance on complementary feeding resulted in first-time mothers reporting increased use of protective feeding practices. These intervention effects were sustained up to 5 years of age and were paralleled by a non-significant trend for lower childhood BMI Z-scores at all post-intervention assessment points. Thus, obesity prevention programs should be continued and maintained during the early childhood years. School age and adolescents: Parents and caregivers are responsible for the type of food presented to young children, the portion sizes offered, and the emotional context in which food is eaten. They should offer appropriate food and food portions, increase fiber intake and decrease sugar intake, foster physical activity, and minimize sedentary behaviors. Dr. Wabistch next explained how high protein intake is a risk factor for later obesity. Increased protein supply leads to increased production of insulin and growth hormone-releasing amino acids (and higher IGF-I levels) which causes rapid weight gain in the first 2 years of life and heightened adipogenic activity leading to adult overweight and obesity. The European Union childhood obesity project ( 44 ) showed that infant formulas with a lower protein content reduce BMI and obesity risk at school age. In the study by Lustig, et al ( 45 ), participants consumed a diet for 9 days to deliver comparable percentages of protein, fat, and carbohydrate as in their self-reported diets; however, dietary sugar was reduced from 28% to 10% and substituted with starch. This isocaloric fructose restriction improved surrogate metabolic parameters (diastolic blood pressure, triglyceride, LDL cholesterol, glucose tolerance, and hyperinsulinemia) in children with obesity and MetS, irrespective of weight change. To conclude, Dr. Wabitsch reported that hyperinsulinemia is a central feature of MetS and is partially caused by pre- and post-natal metabolic programming. Thus, early prevention should aim at reducing pre-conceptional obesity, reducing exaggerated gestational weight gain and diabetes, improving infant formulas, reducing exaggerated weight gain in SGA and AGA infants, and promoting healthy lifestyles of young families. Pediatricians and community preventive services are important for primary prevention. The last speaker, Sonia Caprio, MD (Yale University School of Medicine, New Haven, Connecticut, USA), in her talk entitled: “Current treatment of metabolic syndrome in children”, discussed the role of abdominal fat distribution and cellular mechanisms associated with insulin resistance, and potential lifestyle treatment of MetS in youth. There is cross-talk between white adipose tissue and other tissues in the body via cytokines and hormones as discussed in a review by Kusminski, et al ( 46 ). There are two forms of obesity, one with metabolically healthy white adipose tissue expansion which is associated with an anti-inflammatory state and insulin sensitivity vs. unhealthy white adipose tissue expansion which leads to increased cellular stress and an inflammatory state (increased natural killer cells and macrophages), decreased angiogenesis, and the onset of fibrosis around adipose tissue and eventually insulin resistance and T2DM. There is a marked difference in visceral and subcutaneous abdominal fat despite a similar degree of overall adiposity. In the study by Taskali, et al ( 47 ), a cohort of 118 obese children were stratified based on their ratio of visceral adipose tissue (VAT) to subcutaneous adipose tissue (SAT). Depending on that ratio, there can be a completely different phenotype ranging from insulin sensitivity to insulin resistance with increasing VAT. Molecular studies show that limited or inefficient storage of fat in the subcutaneous tissue is linked to VAT formation, fatty liver, and accumulation of lipids in muscle, which triggers insulin resistance. Her group tested this hypothesis by doing oral glucose tolerance tests (OGTTs) and clamp studies to assess insulin sensitivity; DEXA scans to quantify body composition; and liver, abdominal, and muscle MRIs to measure fat partitioning. In these studies, the distribution of the VAT/VAT+SAT ratio in obese adolescents leads to a bell-shaped curve identifying two groups: one with a low VAT/VAT+SAT ratio (<0.11) and the other with a high VAT/VAT+SAT ratio (>0.11). Longitudinal follow-up over 2–3 years showed that the group with low VAT/VAT+SAT ratio had better insulin sensitivity, and decreased hepatic steatosis, whereas insulin resistance and hepatic steatosis were seen in those with a high VAT/VAT+SAT ratio indicating that this is a persistent condition and the phenotype does not change over time. The central role of fatty liver in the pathogenesis of insulin resistance is described in a study by D’Adamo, et al ( 48 ). In this study, a two-step hyperinsulinemic-euglycemic clamp measured whole body insulin sensitivity: first was the low-dose insulin infusion to assess hepatic insulin production and next was the high-dose insulin infusion to assess muscle sensitivity. A primed continuous infusion of glucose and glycerol tracers was used to quantify insulin effects on glucose turnover and lipolysis. Thirty-eight obese adolescents underwent MRIs and OGTTs. They defined two study groups: one with high liver fat content (>5.5% of fat in the liver on MRI) and other with low liver fat content (<5.5% of liver fat). Both groups were matched for abdominal and muscle fat, but the group with higher liver fat had higher triglyceride levels; mild ALT elevation; higher fasting insulin, HOMA-IR, and c-peptide; and lower adiponectin. During the low-dose insulin infusion in the group with fatty liver, insulin was unable to suppress hepatic glucose production and there was decreased suppression of lipolysis. During high-dose insulin infusion, there was muscle insulin resistance, leading to overall insulin resistance. On longitudinal follow-up with repeat liver MRIs, these children showed no improvement in their fatty liver, again indicating that this is persistent condition. Thus, independent of visceral fat and IMCL, intrahepatic fat accumulation is associated with impaired insulin action in both the liver and muscle, early defects in beta-cell function, and low adiponectin levels. Dr. Caprio next addressed the subject of inflammation in the adipose tissue. Her group measured the inflammatory content (cytokines and their genetic expression by RT-PCR) in biopsied SAT in obese adolescents with high and low VAT/VAT+SAT ratio ( 49 ). After matching for percentage of body fat, those with high VAT/VAT+SAT ratio had impaired adipose cell differentiation, impaired lipogenesis, and a relative reduction in the proportion of large adipose cells. The resulting decreased ability to store triglyceride in SAT is likely an important contributor to the development of liver steatosis and insulin resistance. Thus, in obese adolescents with a healthy liver, there is decreased fatty acid turnover, decreased influx of fatty acids to the liver, and decreased hepatic glucose production, leading to better insulin sensitivity. Lastly, with regard to treatment of MetS, Dr. Caprio’s view is endorsed by the randomized controlled clinical trial by Savoye, et al ( 50 ) who compared two groups: an intervention group with a biweekly family-based exercise, nutrition, and behavior modification program and the other being a control group with clinic-based counseling every 6 months. Both groups were matched for age, pubertal stage, BMI, and baseline biochemical assessments. After 6 months, the intervention group showed a decreased BMI Z-score, decreased body fat, and significant lowering of triglycerides without medication, as opposed to the control group where no difference in these parameters was observed. In summary, she recommended healthy lifestyle changes to prevent MetS as no targeted consistently efficacious treatment has been developed yet. The Controversy session entitled “Imaging for Congenital Hypothyroidism” was co-chaired by Mark Palmert, MD, PhD (Hospital for Sick Children, Toronto, Canada) and Asaf Oren, MD (Tel Aviv University, Tel Aviv, Israel). The first speaker in the debate, Johnny Deladoëy, MD, PhD (Sainte-Justine Hospital and University of Montreal, Montreal, Canada), presented the case in favor of routine thyroid imaging at the time of diagnosis, while Stephen LaFranchi, MD (Oregon Health and Science University, Portland, Oregon) argued against this position. Dr. Deladoëy made the case that thyroid imaging should be a standard step in the newborn screening process once the diagnosis of congenital hypothyroidism (CH) is established biochemically. As an introduction, he heralded screening for CH as a public health triumph. With the addition of CH to newborn screening (NBS) programs, we no longer see decreases in intelligence quotients (IQ) to <70 and their devastating neurological outcomes. Screening for CH achieves the definition of screening of Wilson and Jungner because of the following ( 51 ): The goal of screening for CH is to prevent irreversible intellectual disability The condition of CH is an important heath problem with benefits to early diagnosis An accepted treatment is available as well as facility for confirmation, counseling, and treatment A suitable test to identify CH is available The natural history of CH is understood and there is a recognizable latent or early symptomatic stage It is cost-effective to identify CH As it stands, however, the NBS protocol to detect congenital hypothyroidism is fraught by circular reasoning. Specifically, the biochemical parameters of hypothyroidism are initially identified on a blood spot test that is sent to the NBS laboratory. In order to confirm the diagnosis, another blood test is sent, as opposed to another testing modality. Especially when distinguishing transient from permanent CH, thyroid imaging provides useful additional information. Dr. Deladoëy indicated that there needs to be quality control for NBS programs and that using thyroid imaging would accomplish this. He cited the 20-year retrospective study examining cases of CH in Quebec ( 52 ). After 2001, the TSH cut-off was decreased from 15 to 5 mIU/L on the second newborn screen test after the TSH was noted to be intermediate on the first test (15–30 mIU/L). With this change in the TSH cut-off, the additional cases of CH identified were noted to be functional disorders of the thyroid gland. These milder cases of CH mostly resulted in permanent CH, and the impact of these functional disorders of the thyroid gland, also referred to as normal-sized gland in situ disorders, on intellectual disability is unknown. Instead of relying on biochemical assays to delineate the permanence of CH for these mild or uncertain cases, Dr. Deladoëy argued that using thyroid imaging can be helpful. He also highlighted that mild neonatal hyperthyrotropinemia (MNH), as defined by mildly elevated TSH and normal free thyroxine levels, is increasing in incidence. Additional information from thyroid scans would be beneficial in these borderline cases. He summarized that “cherry-picking” only special cases is not an option for quality control, and that thyroid imaging should be implemented in a systematic manner. Dr. Deladoëy then stated that the thyroid scan is accurate, available, timely, safe, and cost-effective, and helps to tailor treatment duration to avoid unnecessary long-term treatment. The results of a technetium 99m (Tc99m) scan serve as a visual decision aid for providers and informs decisions to perform DNA-based tests such as gene sequencing to rule out inherited forms of CH. It also serves as an educational tool for parents to confirm the diagnosis of CH. Dr. Deladoëy purported that thyroid ultrasound is not a suitable replacement for Tc99m scan because it is poor at identifying ectopy even with Doppler technology. He also reviewed that nuclear medicine services are widely available (284 centers in Canada and 5000 centers in the US). The cost of imaging is reasonable as well (1 mCi of Tc99m is $0.62 USD and the technical cost is $65.5 USD). He emphasized the minimal radiation risk of Tc99m stating that the exposure is equivalent to one-third of a chest radiograph, one round-trip transcontinental flight, and living one month at sea level ( 53 ). Thyroid scans also allow providers to determine the etiology of CH in order to individualize the prognosis. For example, he stated that prognosis of CH due to athyreosis should be tightly guarded as these patients have, on average, a 5-point lower IQ compared to those with ectopy. Therefore, a specific diagnosis may change how providers titrate the levothyroxine dose. Moreover, if a thyroid scan is consistent with dyshormonogenesis, additional genetic counseling may be necessary as this form of CH is inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion. Dr. Deladoëy shared an anecdote of a 9-year-old patient with a missed lingual thyroid that was previously evaluated by ultrasound. The mother was not convinced about the ectopic thyroid, and stopped her child’s levothyroxine. This was followed by a TSH increase to 30 mIU/L and an increase in thyroid tissue mass that became obvious to the patient and mother on visual inspection. Having thyroid scan data from the start may have increased parental acceptance of the diagnosis and improved compliance. In closing, Dr. Deladoëy emphasized that NBS to identify infants with CH is a dynamic process that would be improved by routine thyroid imaging, specifically radionucleotide thyroid scans. These would improve the diagnostic certainty, reduce circular confirmatory testing, and serve as an available, timely, cost-effective, and safe testing modality. He also emphasized thyroid scans as a tool to convince parents of the reality of CH in the hope of improving treatment compliance. He alluded to the possible costs of over-diagnosis and unnecessary treatment as a consequence of routine thyroid imaging, but he left this discussion open-ended. For the opposing view, Dr. LaFranchi began by conducting an informal show-of-hands poll of the audience, of whom about half routinely performed thyroid imaging at the time of diagnosis of CH and, of those, about half obtained radionucleotide imaging. Based on a survey from pediatric endocrinologists in Texas, Arkansas, and Oklahoma, providers with more than 15 years of experience “usually” or “always” recommended thyroid imaging for evaluation for CH more so than those with less than 15 years of experience ( 54 ). Dr. LaFranchi also set the stage by highlighting the society guidelines from the American Academic of Pediatrics (AAP) and the Pediatric Endocrine Society (PES) which both state that thyroid ultrasound and scanning are optional diagnostic studies in evaluation of CH ( 55 ). To build his case, Dr. LaFranchi answered a series of questions related to thyroid imaging in the evaluation of CH. First, “Does imaging guide the selection of the initial levothyroxine dose?” He presented the case of a 7-day-old infant with a low total thyroxine level of 4.5 μg/dL and very elevated TSH of 623 mIU/L. Repeat testing showed the free thyroxine was low at 0.4 ng/dL and TSH elevated to 548 mIU/L. Routine imaging with Tc99m was completed and showed thyroid hypoplasia. The starting dose of levothyroxine was 15 mcg/kg/day, which was not influenced by the thyroid scan results. He then reviewed the AAP/PES/European Society of Pediatric Endocrinology (ESPE) guidelines for CH which describe the starting dose range of levothyroxine of 10–15 mcg/kg/day with the caveat to start at the higher end of the range if the free thyroxine is below the normal range or if there is a severe elevation of TSH ( 55 , 56 ). In the case of moderate hypothyroidism with a free thyroxine of 1.2 ng/dL and TSH of 30 mIU/L, opting for a levothyroxine dose closer to 10 mcg/kg/day followed by close laboratory monitoring may be as or more important than thyroid imaging. Dr. LaFranchi’s second question was: “Does thyroid imaging facilitate genetic counseling for recurrence risk?” He presented a case of a newborn with a goiter, total thyroxine of 4.6 μg/dL and TSH of 80 mIU/L, with subsequent results showing a free thyroxine of 0.9 ng/dL and TSH 60 mIU/L. The thyroid scan in this case showed increased uptake that was consistent with dyshormonogenesis which carries a 1 in 4 recurrence risk. He argued that having the thyroid scan result is only helpful if the family plans on having a subsequent child. If there is subsequent child, he recommends obtaining thyroid function studies on the second day of life for any future siblings in order to identify CH. His third question was: “Does thyroid imaging predict permanent vs. transient congenital hypothyroidism?” As reviewed in the AAP/PES/ESPE guidelines, re-evaluation of thyroid function is recommended after 3 years of age if the etiology is not determined in the neonatal period ( 55 , 56 ). He argued that thyroid imaging does not always provide a definite etiology, as complete lack of uptake occurs in both thyroid aplasia (permanent) and in cases of transplacental passage of maternal TSH-receptor blocking antibodies (transient). The guidelines recommend that, after 3 years, every child with CH should be trialed off levothyroxine for 4–6 weeks and free T4 and TSH should be rechecked. If the TSH is > 10 mIU/L, the patient will have permanent CH. Dr. LaFranchi’s fourth question was: “Does thyroid imaging guide management in the cases of borderline congenital hypothyroidism?” He presented another case of congenital hypothyroidism in a newborn with an initial TSH on NBS of 45.88 mIU/L, which, by the third NBS, decreased to 15.32 mIU/L. At 28 days of life, TSH was 10.08 mIU/L and free thyroxine was normal at 1.2 ng/dL. At 35 days of life, the TSH was 11.5 mIU/L and the free thyroxine was again normal at 1.1 ng/dL. An ultrasound was done which showed a eutopic gland in situ . This patient was started on treatment with levothyroxine and trialed off at 24 months, but was subsequently diagnosed with permanent CH. He stated that imaging did not make a difference in this case. The fifth and final question was: “Does thyroid imaging improve treatment compliance?” He cited an article that looked at filled prescriptions for thyroid hormone replacement among patients with CH ( 57 ). About one-third of patients had discontinued treatment within 36 months, which is not consistent with recommendations, indicating that a diagnosis of CH may be associated with medication compliance. He mentioned that, in a provider survey ( 54 ), determining a specific etiology of CH can help offer definitive answers and genetic counseling, but focusing efforts on daily levothyroxine dosing, thyroid blood test monitoring, and medication refills is arguably more important. Dr. LaFranchi concluded by stating that he is not universally opposed to thyroid imaging for evaluation of CH, but felt that its use should be cautious and selective. He implored the audience to consider when ordering any test, to ask themselves how the results will help with management of the patient. In other words, “Ordering a laboratory test is like ‘picking your nose’: if you find something, you better know what to do with it.” This was a riveting session and, while there may not have been a clear “winner,” the audience left better informed about the pros and cons of obtaining routine thyroid imaging in the initial evaluation of children with congenital hypothyroidism. Footnotes Disclosure The authors have nothing to declare References 1. Nunley KA, Rosano C, Ryan CM, Jennings JR, Aizenstein HJ, Zgibor JC, Costacou T, Boudreau RM, Miller R, Orchard TJ, Saxton JA. Clinically Relevant Cognitive Impairment in Middle-Aged Adults With Childhood-Onset Type 1 Diabetes. Diabetes Care 2015;38:1768–1776 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Biessels GJ, Reijmer YD. Brain changes underlying cognitive dysfunction in diabetes: what can we learn from MRI? Diabetes 2014;63:2244–2252 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Gonder-Frederick LA, Zrebiec JF, Bauchowitz AU, Ritterband LM, Magee JC, Cox DJ, Clarke WL. Cognitive function is disrupted by both hypo- and hyperglycemia in school-aged children with type 1 diabetes: a field study. Diabetes Care 2009;32:1001–1006 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Deary IJ, Crawford JR, Hepburn DA, Langan SJ, Blackmore LM, Frier BM. Severe hypoglycemia and intelligence in adult patients with insulin-treated diabetes. Diabetes 1993;42:341–344 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Aye T, Reiss AL, Kesler S, Hoang S, Drobny J, Park Y, Schleifer K, Baumgartner H, Wilson DM, Buckingham BA. The feasibility of detecting neuropsychologic and neuroanatomic effects of type 1 diabetes in y