2004 , preface). This research builds upon animal studies in endocrinology and behavior and brings them closer to the fields of human psychology and psychiatry. Other researchers have claimed to have identified cognitive differences in animals caused by hormonal changes and have created animal models for well-known psychiatric problems such as depression and schizophrenia, for example, the work of Knut Larsson (Ågmo & Vega Matuszczyk, 2003 ). Although researchers such as Cohen have focused on hormones and sexual difference; other neuroendocrinologists have examined nonsex-specific topics such as the endocrinology of stress responses (Romero & Butler, 2007 ). Journals such as Frontiers in Neuroendocrinology (1969–present) and Hormones and Behavior (1969–present) have distributed this work which is increasingly being integrated into the broader field of neuroscience. Several recent studies have shown that the isolated thyroid hormone, thyroxin, appears to augment antidepressants in resistant depression ( Aronson et al., 1996 ; Joffe & Sokolov, 2000 ). Furthermore, recent research in neuroscience suggests that dopamine and serotonin levels are correlated with pituitary–thyroid state ( Strawn et al., 2004 ). Finally, controversial new research is suggesting that estradiol can also augment antidepressants in the treatment of female depression ( Morgan et al., 2005 ). References Ågmo A, Vega Matuszczyk J. From rat sexual behavior to schizophrenia: A tribute to Professor Emeritus Knut Larsson. Scandinavian Journal of Psychology. 2003;44(3):169–171. doi: 10.1111/1467-9450.t01-1-00334. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Allderidge P. The foundation of the Maudsley Hospital. In: Berrios GE, Freeman H, editors. 150 years of British psychiatry, 1841–1991. London: Gaskell; 1991. p. 420. [ Google Scholar ] Anon. Reports and analytical records from the Lancet Laboratory. The Lancet. 1913;182(4689):84–85. [ Google Scholar ] Anon. Courses in psychiatry. British Medical Journal. 1